\page advanced Advanced

Edge-Device signal processing necessitates efficient management of resources. The big data nature of CSI processing forces
ones to think not only in the user-space, but also in the hardware level. In achieving seamless processing, utilization of
the underlying hardware platform plays an important role. To make this possible, VesperCSI is equipped with kernel-level
configurability.


---



## Brief Introduction to Internals

- VesperCSI contains two main threads (called `runners` in general) other than the main thread. These are called `capture` and `consume`.
- `capture` thread (runner) takes the CSI data from Netlink buffer and append it to the internal ring buffer.
- `consume` thread (runner) processes the CSI data as desired, including but not limited to writing the data to a file.
- VesperCSI allows one to configure these threads to run according to defined rules.


---

## Thread Affinity & Priorities

**Thread Affinity**

- Each runner can be configured to run inside a specific CPU core, decreasing the time it takes the system to decide which core to run the `runner` on.

**Priority**

- With the help of a Real-Time Linux Kernel, a priority number can be set to prevent any other process from running inside that CPU core.

> If you are not sure whether you successfully prevented every user-space process and kernel threads from running inside 
> the cores that `runners` use (`CPU Core isloation`), do not use this feature.


---

